+# Gaps: current capture against the billing team's statement
+
+Evidence: captures/statement.txt is present and readable. It has 26 lines, the
+widest is 64 characters, and it has no tabs, no trailing spaces and no
+non-ASCII characters, as captures/CAPTURE.md records. CAPTURE.md says it was
+rendered at 2026-10-04T22:24:47Z from src/statement.mjs (sha256 0020349d84b1).
+The file timestamps match. I could not rerun the hash in this session, so the
+evidence is accepted as the current revision. Both statements are for Northwind
+Robotics, NW-20418, September 2026.
+
+## Side by side, labels stripped
+
+The labels were set by a parity draw: the millisecond field of the capture's
+render time (.859) is odd, so the capture became A. The two statements were
+compared as A and B against the seven points before the labels were revealed.
+
+| What matters | Statement A | Statement B |
+|---|---|---|
+| Total found at once | Charges, then a "Total charges" subtotal, then credits, then "Total due" 3,580.78 in the amount column between `=` rules | Charges, then a "Charges" subtotal, then credits, then a `-` rule and "Total due" 3,580.78 in the amount column. Both are equally easy to find |
+| Largest first | 1,642.50 down to 36.00. The 36.00 tie is ordered by name (Key management, then Load balancing). Credits run 410.63, then 164.25 | Same order for the charges and for the credits |
+| Amounts read as money | Every amount has commas and cents. Every decimal point is in column 61, including the credits and the totals. Credits are written `(410.63)` with the `)` past the digit column. "USD" appears only in the heading | Same. The decimal points share one column, credits use parentheses, and "USD" appears only in the heading |
+| Nothing that says nothing | No zero-usage rows. A two-line sentence at the foot gives the count and names both services | Same sentence, same wrap |
+| Each charge's share | Share column at one decimal, aligned on the `%`, with 100.0% on the subtotal. The rounded values add to 100.0 | Same values |
+| Month by name; header | "Northwind Robotics" opens line 1 with "Account NW-20418" at the right edge. "September 2026" is spelled out | Same |
+| Usage with its unit | Quantity right-aligned with the unit beside it ("18,250 vCPU-hours"), so the thousands line up | Quantity and unit left-aligned as one phrase ("18,250 vCPU-hours") |
+
+Arithmetic in A, checked by hand: the charges add to 4,155.66. Then
+4,155.66 - 410.63 - 164.25 = 3,580.78. Each share recomputes to the value shown,
+for example 1,642.50 / 4,155.66 = 39.52%, 209.76 / 4,155.66 = 5.05% and
+36.00 / 4,155.66 = 0.87%.
+
+**Better: neither.** A is the capture and B is the billing team's statement.
+On all seven points they match. The rows, the order, the figures, the shares,
+the credits and the footnote are the same. The two differ only in widths and
+wording:
+- A is 64 characters wide and B is 69.
+- A's usage column right-aligns the quantities. This arguably reads better,
+  because the thousands line up.
+- A labels the subtotal "Total charges" where B says "Charges".
+- A sets off the total due with `=` rules above and below. B uses one `-`
+  rule.
+
+The yardstick says rule length, column width and heading wording are not
+gaps unless they break one of its points, and none of these does. A still
+ends on the total due, set off by a rule, with the amount in the amount
+column.
+
+## Ranked gaps
+
+None. The capture now has none of the problems the yardstick calls major:
+- Charges and credits are in amount order.
+- A subtotal line adds up the charges, and the credits come after it.
+- The decimal points line up.
+- Thousands have commas.
+- No amount has a minus sign or a currency sign.
+- No zero-usage rows.
+- The month is spelled out.
+- The share column is there.
+
+There is no minor gap worth asking the owner to act on either.
+
+Not counted as gaps:
+- "Total charges" where the reference says "Charges"
+- the double `=` rules around the total due, and the blank line before them
+- the right-aligned usage quantities
+- the 64-character width
+
+These are wording and width choices that break none of the seven points.
+
+verdict: pass
